TI  - Watch-And-Help: A Challenge for Social Perception and Human-AI Collaboration
AU  - Xavier Puig
AU  - Tianmin Shu
AU  - Shuang Li
AU  - Zilin Wang
AU  - Yuan-Hong Liao
AU  - Joshua B Tenenbaum
AU  - Sanja Fidler
AU  - Antonio Torralba
PY  - 2021
T2  - International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR 2021)
AB  - In this paper, we introduce Watch-And-Help (WAH), a challenge for testing social intelligence in agents. In WAH, an AI agent needs to help a human-like agent perform a complex household task efficiently. To succeed, the AI agent needs to i) understand the underlying goal of the task by watching a single demonstration of the human-like agent performing the same task (social perception), and ii) coordinate with the human-like agent to solve the task in an unseen environment as fast as possible (human-AI collaboration). For this challenge, we build VirtualHome-Social, a multi-agent household environment, and provide a benchmark including both planning and learning based baselines. We evaluate the performance of AI agents with the human-like agent as well as and with real humans using objective metrics and subjective user ratings. Experimental results demonstrate that our challenge and virtual environment enable a systematic evaluation on the important aspects of machine social intelligence at scale.
